 ],
 "literal": "O pot-making potter! O pot-making potter! / Like a small white lizard attached to the spoke of a wheeled cart, with him / who came through many desert paths, showing pity to me also, / for this burial urn of the wide, broad world, / please shape it so that it is wide, / O pot-making potter of the wide, ancient town!",
 "translation": "O potter who makes urns! O potter who makes urns! Like a tiny white lizard clinging to the spoke of a wheeled cart, I have traversed many desert paths with him. Show pity to me too: make the burial urn wide enough for both of us, O potter of the ancient, wide-spaced town!",
 "modern": "பாண்டங்களைச் செய்யும் குயவனே! பாண்டங்களைச் செய்யும் குயவனே! அச்சுடைய வண்டியின் ஆரக்காலில் ஒட்டியிருக்கும் சிறிய வெண்மையான பல்லியைப் போல, அவனுடனே பல பாலைநில வழிகளைக் கடந்து வந்த எனக்கும் இரக்கம் காட்டி, இந்த அகன்ற உலகத்தில் அவனுக்காகச் செய்யும் ஈமத்தாழியை எனக்கும் சேர்த்துப் பெரியதாக வனைவாயாக, அகன்ற இடங்களைக் கொண்ட பழமையான ஊரின் குயவனே!",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by a grieving wife to a potter in an ancient town after her husband's death. She asks the potter to make the burial urn wide enough to accommodate her alongside her deceased husband. To express her deep bond, she compares herself to a tiny white lizard that clings to the spoke of a cartwheel, traveling wherever it goes. She has followed him through many difficult desert paths in life and wishes to remain with him in death. This poignant request reflects the theme of 'mutupālai', depicting the ultimate path of no return. The key words are 'kalamcey kōvē' (O potter) and 'īmat tāḻi' (burial urn).",
